#. type: Title #
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:1 src/en/reference/glossary.md:6
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Glossary"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:10
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Ever wondered what we mean by a specific term? We've been collecting the "
"most relevant terms below for your insight."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:12
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<VPGlossary>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:19
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Admin\n"
": An admin(istrator) is a user in Zammad who has special rights and manages "
"your\n"
"  Zammad. An admin can, for example, configure user permissions, time "
"recording\n"
"  settings, automation and text modules.\n"
"  If you're looking for someone to adjust anything in your Zammad, your "
"admin\n"
"  is the right person to ask for.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:24
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Agent\n"
": An agent is a user in Zammad who works on tickets and responds to "
"customer\n"
"  inquiries. Agents can be admins too, meaning that they can change "
"settings,\n"
"  user permissions and so on (see above).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:30
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"API\n"
": An API (Application Programming Interface) is a set of rules and "
"protocols\n"
"  that allow different software applications to communicate with each "
"other.\n"
"  It allows you to connect third-party systems with your Zammad "
"(e.g. social\n"
"  media, messengers, monitoring tools).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:34
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  You can learn more on our\n"
"  [API landing "
"page](https://zammad.com/en/product/features/rest-api){target=_blank}\n"
"  as well as in our [API reference](/en/reference/rest-api/intro).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:40
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Article\n"
": Each item within a ticket is called article. Ticket articles\n"
"  can be internal (only agents can see them) or public\n"
"  (customers can see them too; e.g. public notes or outgoing emails) and "
"include\n"
"  formatted text as well as attachments and inline images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:48
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Automation\n"
": There are many processes that can be automated with Zammad.\n"
"  This means that certain actions take place automatically.\n"
"  An automation can be time-based or action-based and should cover every\n"
"  conceivable automation scenario.\n"
"  An example could be the recurring deletion of customers who are no "
"longer\n"
"  needed or the assignment of groups based on ticket attributes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:54
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Autosave\n"
": Zammad's autosave feature makes sure that no work is lost. So if your "
"browser\n"
"  crashes, simply log in again and resume where you were before.\n"
"  Learn more on our\n"
"  [Autosave landing "
"page](https://zammad.com/en/product/features/autosave){target=_blank}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:58
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Branding\n"
": Every company has a different identity. This is why Zammad lets you add "
"your\n"
"  logo, signature, links and other things!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:62
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Changelog\n"
": With every new release comes a new changelog. It is basically a list of "
"all\n"
"  the things that have changed, from new enhancements to bug fixes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:65
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  You can find them all on our\n"
"  "
"[GitHub](https://github.com/zammad/zammad/blob/stable/CHANGELOG.md){target=_blank}!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:71
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Checkmk\n"
": Checkmk is a powerful IT monitoring tool that can send real-time status\n"
"  alerts to Zammad via email or REST API. Set these alerts up in Checkmk,\n"
"  and Zammad will automatically create, update and close tickets based on\n"
"  the health of your system.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:77
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Clearbit\n"
": Clearbit is a service designed to collect information on your\n"
"  contacts. Thus, new queries from unknown users in Zammad can be "
"automatically\n"
"  enriched with information such as company, number of employees,\n"
"  annual turnover, industry and much more.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:84
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Conflict Warning\n"
": When two agents edit the same ticket at the same time, a lot can go wrong "
"-\n"
"  from duplicate replies to overwritten messages. Zammad helps you to "
"avoid\n"
"  this with its integrated conflict warning. So if you see another agent's\n"
"  avatar and a little pen at the bottom of your ticket, it means the other "
"agent\n"
"  is currently editing it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:88
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Content Sidebar\n"
": Right sidebar in the ticket view which includes the sidebar tabs and the\n"
"  active sidebar (if opened).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:94
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Core Workflows\n"
": This feature allows you to configure dynamic fields and ticket masks based "
"on\n"
"  attribute available in Zammad. For example you can disable or hide "
"fields,\n"
"  make them visible based on other fields or attributes, make them mandatory "
"and\n"
"  much more!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:100
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"CTI\n"
": CTI stands for Computer Telephony Integration and allows you to collect\n"
"  detailed information on all your incoming and outgoing calls.\n"
"  This includes, for example, a call log, an overview of which agent is\n"
"  currently on a call, a caller ID search, and even a Do-Not-Disturb-Mode.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:109
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Custom Development (CD)\n"
": We are constantly working on improving Zammad, and we keep adding new\n"
"  features with every single release. However, sometimes our customers "
"might\n"
"  require a very specific new feature, addition, or adjustment that is "
"either\n"
"  very urgent or very particular to their individual use case.\n"
"  This is when a custom development can take place: We offer the customer "
"to\n"
"  develop the desired feature at a price that we agree upon previously\n"
"  (which is based on the expected hours needed for completion).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:117
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Customer\n"
": A customer is a person you communicate with via Zammad.\n"
"  For each customer there is a profile page showing all the tickets and "
"more\n"
"  information. Customers can be assigned to one or more organizations.\n"
"  Each customer can access their individual\n"
"  Customer Interface, where they see all their tickets with the current\n"
"  status and live updates.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:124
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Dashboard\n"
": The dashboard is the individual welcome page in Zammad and gives\n"
"  you feedback about your work.\n"
"  Here you can find all sorts of overviews, such as the open tickets,\n"
"  the average waiting time, or the reopening rate. You can also see what "
"your\n"
"  colleagues are doing by checking the Activity Stream.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:129
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Dialog\n"
": A dialog is a element which pops up in the middle of the screen because of "
"its\n"
"  importance. An example could be the dialog which asks you to save or "
"discard\n"
"  your unsaved changes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:133
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Documentation\n"
": You will probably have noticed already: you are reading a part of "
"Zammad's\n"
"  documentation. 😉\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:139
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Elasticsearch\n"
": Zammad offers an Elasticsearch integration (a free and open search "
"engine)\n"
"  that makes the search process within Zammad super fast and powerful. You\n"
"  can even connect a reporting tool like Grafana to your Elasticsearch to\n"
"  create powerful reports and statistics.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:144
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Escalation\n"
": An escalation is what happens after the deadline for a ticket has "
"passed. You\n"
"  can configure it with Zammad's SLAs. An escalated ticket is marked red in "
"your\n"
"  taskbar and the overviews.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:150
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Exchange Integration\n"
": The Exchange integration allows you to sync your contacts from your\n"
"  Exchange address book to Zammad. Every time a contact is updated\n"
"  in Exchange, it will be reflected in Zammad. This gives you direct\n"
"  access to all your contacts in your Zammad.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:157
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"External Authentication\n"
": External authentication is an easy, one-click option for your users to "
"log\n"
"  into Zammad. It has various benefits: not only is it faster but it also\n"
"  means that your users will have to remember fewer passwords.\n"
"  Zammad currently supports more than ten login providers, such as "
"Facebook,\n"
"  GitHub, GitLab, Google, or Microsoft / Office365.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:162
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Feature\n"
": A feature is a specific capability or functionality that Zammad provides "
"to\n"
"  you such as our integrations, productivity tools or additional channels.\n"
"  We keep adding new features with every release.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:169
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Feature Request\n"
": You can let us know if you are missing a particular feature in Zammad.\n"
"  We collect all of your wishes in our community forum in the\n"
"  [feature request "
"category](https://community.zammad.org/c/stuff-you-like-zammad-to-have-feel-free-to-discuss-and-add-proposals/6/all){target=_blank}.\n"
"  If a request comes in regularly and we think that it would be a\n"
"  great addition, we'll put it on our roadmap and start working on it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:174
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Feature Sponsoring\n"
": If an organization urgently requires one of the features on the list,\n"
"  they can fast-forward the development and put it on top of the list by\n"
"  sponsoring it, which means that they cover the costs for the "
"development.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:179
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"GitHub\n"
": GitHub is a service for the version management of software development\n"
"  projects. It uses Git, a software that tracks changes in file sets.\n"
"  Here at Zammad, we use it to maintain our repository.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:183
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  As Zammad is an open-source project, many developers and tech-lovers from "
"all\n"
"  over the world contribute to it. GitHub is where we coordinate all of "
"this.\n"
"  You can find the repository "
"[here](https://github.com/zammad/zammad){target=_blank}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:187
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Besides our own repo, Zammad also has an integration for GitHub.\n"
"  It creates a data exchange that shows you all relevant information about "
"your\n"
"  issues directly in a sidebar tab, such as status or assignees.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:191
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"GitLab\n"
": GitLab is similar to GitHub.\n"
"  Here at Zammad, we use it for our internal development.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:195
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  There is also an integration that allows users to connect GitLab to "
"Zammad\n"
"  so that all their issues and their corresponding changes are reflected "
"in\n"
"  Zammad's sidebar tab as well.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:200
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Grafana\n"
": Grafana is an open-source reporting tool.\n"
"  Zammad users on the Plus plan can integrate it into their instance and\n"
"  receive detailed analytics on their performance.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:203
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Hooking up Grafana to Elasticsearch is an possibility you can do on your\n"
"  on-premise installation as well.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:212
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Groups\n"
": Groups are a synonym for departments or processing groups.\n"
"  Incoming tickets can be assigned to groups.\n"
"  Within the group, an owner can be defined, who is then responsible for\n"
"  this ticket. The access to tickets is controlled via the roles. In a role "
"you\n"
"  can define which permission it has based on the different groups.\n"
"  The possible permissions are \"read\", \"create\", \"change\", "
"\"overview\" and\n"
"  \"full\" (or none of them).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:216
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  If you have worked with the OTRS system in the past, you might remember "
"the\n"
"  principle of \"queues\". The groups in Zammad are the same as the queues "
"in\n"
"  OTRS.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:222
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Icinga\n"
": Icinga is a monitoring system that monitors the availability of an\n"
"  organization's entire system infrastructure.\n"
"  It can be integrated into Zammad so that it triggers a ticket in case\n"
"  of a warning situation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:230
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"i-doit\n"
": i-doit is a CMDB (Configuration Management Data Base).\n"
"  It helps you to keep an eye on every piece of the physical and digital\n"
"  infrastructure. A corresponding integration makes it possible to connect\n"
"  it to Zammad, where it adds a new tab to Zammad's ticket sidebar so\n"
"  you can link to existing i-doit objects for easy reference.\n"
"  It also allows you to create Zammad tickets in i-doit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:234
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Issue-Tracking System\n"
": Issue trackers are usually systems that track processes on a technical "
"level.\n"
"  Two of the best-known examples are GitHub and GitLab.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:238
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Zammad is also often referred to as an issue-tracking system.\n"
"  However, as a helpdesk, it focuses on communication at the customer\n"
"  level rather than the technical level.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:243
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Kibana <Badge type=\"info\" text=\"on-premise only\" />\n"
": Kibana is a browser-based open-source reporting tool that focuses on "
"data\n"
"  evaluation. It was developed by Elastic, which is why it is not a "
"surprise\n"
"  that it uses data from Elasticsearch for its analytics.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:246
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Kibana can be integrated with Zammad, allowing for helpdesk data to be\n"
"  mapped in the reporting tool.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:251
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Knowledge Base\n"
": Think of a very extensive set of FAQs - that's exactly what the\n"
"  Zammad knowledge base is. It collects all important information:\n"
"  definitions, processes, how-tos, organizational charts, etc.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:256
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Knowledge base articles can be either internal or external, so you can\n"
"  either show them to the world (good for information on your product or\n"
"  service, for example) or keep them for your team (e.g. for internal "
"processes\n"
"  or team info).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:261
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"LDAP\n"
": A Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) helps provide information\n"
"  about your users within Zammad. Authentication of users against the LDAP\n"
"  and LDAP role mapping to Zammad roles are also possible.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:267
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Macro\n"
": A macro is a series of actions. By starting the macro, the actions are\n"
"  triggered and executed, so users don't have to work through each\n"
"  individual step separately. It saves an enormous amount of time and "
"ensures\n"
"  that no step is forgotten.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:270
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  You can define your own macros and perform actions based on all "
"available\n"
"  attributes in the ticket.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:275
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Mentions\n"
": Mentions are a Zammad feature that allows you to tag another agent in a\n"
"  ticket. Just type `@@` and the name. The selected person will be "
"notified\n"
"  and will be watching the ticket from now on.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:281
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Migrator / Migration Wizard\n"
": If a company wants to switch from another helpdesk software to Zammad,\n"
"  they often have one concern: What about their existing data?\n"
"  That's why we have built our migration wizards that help with migrating "
"all\n"
"  data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:286
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Monit\n"
": Monit is an open-source monitoring tool that relies on a simple setup "
"and\n"
"  a strong community. You can integrate it with Zammad - this way,\n"
"  a ticket is created every time you receive an email in Monit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:290
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Navigation\n"
": Navigation means the left sidebar in Zammad which contains navigation "
"tabs,\n"
"  the search, notifications and many more items, depending on your system.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:294
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Navigation Tab\n"
": One element of the navigation. You can have multiple tabs open and "
"quickly\n"
"  switch between them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:299
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Nagios\n"
": Nagios is another monitoring tool that alerts IT teams when an incident\n"
"  happens. It can be integrated with Zammad so that a ticket is created in\n"
"  case of an alert.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:304
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"On-Premise\n"
": This term means that Zammad is not provided as a cloud service of the\n"
"  company Zammad, but runs on a server which is controlled by a user or\n"
"  customer of Zammad. Self hosting means the same.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:310
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Organization\n"
": An organization is a grouping of customers that operate within the same\n"
"  company or company group.\n"
"  A customer, whose organization is \"sharing\", has access to all tickets "
"of its\n"
"  organization.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:316
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Owner\n"
": The owner of a ticket is the person responsible for and working on it.\n"
"  The owner can be changed to another agent.\n"
"  In this case, it is recommended to leave a handover note in the ticket "
"so\n"
"  that the new owner knows what to do.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:322
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Parent/Child Relationship\n"
": If one ticket results in other subtasks (or additional correspondences),\n"
"  you can split it into several tickets. The main one will then be the "
"parent\n"
"  ticket and the tickets with related subtopics are children.\n"
"  By the way: in the same way, you can also merge two tickets into one.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:328
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Placetel\n"
": Placetel is a Cloud Telephone System that allows users to make phone "
"calls\n"
"  via VoIP. Use your regular phone number and call someone directly on "
"their\n"
"  mobile or landline while still having all communication in one place\n"
"  in Zammad.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:333
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Integrating a Placetel account with Zammad provides users with a call "
"log,\n"
"  making the history of their correspondences even more accurate.\n"
"  Callers are identified directly by their caller ID - a key function that\n"
"  saves agents a lot of time when assigning callers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:338
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Priority\n"
": Every ticket gets assigned a priority. By default, the priority is 2 "
"(normal).\n"
"  Additionally there are the priorities 1 (low) and 3 (high). You can even\n"
"  add more priorities to your Zammad.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:346
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Release\n"
": Every few months, we release a new version of Zammad which is called a\n"
"  release. Every release adds new features to our software. There are major "
"and\n"
"  minor releases:\n"
"  major releases (such as Zammad 1.0, 2.0, etc.) bring major changes.\n"
"  Minor releases (such as 1.1, 2.1, etc.) are released in the meantime and "
"bring\n"
"  smaller updates.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:350
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  In our [release notes](https://zammad.com/en/releases){target=_blank} you "
"can\n"
"  find what features are included in new versions and also important "
"information\n"
"  about what to consider before updating Zammad.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:354
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Role\n"
": Every user has an assigned role. By default there are three roles:\n"
"  admin, agent, and customer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:361
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  _Admins_ have the most rights: they can define roles, permissions,\n"
"  and settings for the entire team and instance.\n"
"  _Agents_ can view and edit tickets, but not change any settings other "
"than\n"
"  those of their own profiles.\n"
"  _Customers_ can view their ticket's processing status in their\n"
"  customer interface.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:363
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "  You can even add new roles to your Zammad.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:367
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Second Level Navigation\n"
": Refers to an additional menu next to the navigation (e.g. when opening "
"profile\n"
"  settings).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:371
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Side(Panel)\n"
": This is the panel which is flying in on the right side for actions like "
"ticket\n"
"  linking or ticket merging.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:376
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Sidebar Tab\n"
": In the content sidebar, there are different tabs like \"Customer\",\n"
"  \"Organization\" and \"Ticket\", depending on your system and "
"ticket. These are\n"
"  called sidebar tabs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:378
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "  An active sidebar tab is the currently opened sidebar tab.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:384
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Sipgate\n"
": Sipgate is a SaaS solution for internet telephony.\n"
"  The Zammad integration for Sipgate provides users with a detailed call\n"
"  overview. If you have a customer that wants to get connected to a "
"certain\n"
"  agent, the caller log will tell you if this colleague is currently "
"available.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:391
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"SLA\n"
": A Service Level Agreement (SLA) is a contract between an end-user and a\n"
"  company that defines the minimum expected service requirements including\n"
"  quality, availability, and punctuality.\n"
"  They are used to set expectations and hold companies accountable for "
"keeping\n"
"  their promises.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:395
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  You can easily set up SLAs in Zammad and define parameters such as the "
"time\n"
"  for the first response, an update, and a solution. Once the deadline has "
"been\n"
"  reached, the ticket will escalate.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:400
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"S/MIME\n"
": S/MIME is the most widely-supported method for secure email "
"communication.\n"
"  By activating it in Zammad, all messages sent from Zammad will be signed "
"and\n"
"  encrypted.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:405
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"SSO\n"
": Single-sign-on (SSO) allows you to access all your systems and devices "
"with\n"
"  just one login. There are various providers that make this process easy "
"and\n"
"  secure. Zammad currently supports SSO via SAML and Shibboleth.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:412
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"State\n"
": Every ticket has a state which reflects the current progress of resolving "
"the\n"
"  issue. The state of a ticket can be changed manually or automatically "
"based\n"
"  on automation or SLA. Each state has a state types. There are four state "
"types\n"
"  by default and they are color-coded. You can even add additional states "
"to\n"
"  your Zammad.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:420
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Tags\n"
": Tags help you to categorize tickets. You can define them based on your "
"use\n"
"  case. For example, if you're a retail business, your tags could be based "
"on\n"
"  your product categories to help you organize tickets by the type of "
"product\n"
"  they refer to.\n"
"  But they could also be based on the type of request, e.g. refund, "
"delivery\n"
"  issue, complaint.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:423
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Tags can be queried in conditions in an automation and also assigned\n"
"  automatically, for example depending on keywords in the ticket title.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:431
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Text Module\n"
": If you find that you send the same answers over and over again,\n"
"  you can save yourself a lot of work and create a text module.\n"
"  This way, you just need to type the `::` shortcut in an article and all\n"
"  available text modules are shown from which you can select the desired "
"one.\n"
"  You can limit the search results by adding parts of the text module's "
"name,\n"
"  keywords or content after the `::`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:434
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  For example, here at Zammad, we have a text module with the shortcut\n"
"  `::ilff`, which turns into `I look forward to your feedback`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:439
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(Ticket) Template\n"
": If you create many similar tickets, you can create a template for "
"them. This\n"
"  is helpful for introductions to your product/service or for drawing up "
"an\n"
"  offer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:445
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"User\n"
": A user is any user of the ticket system. Each user has assigned\n"
"  permissions, which allow them to access certain areas and information.\n"
"  Users can have various roles, with the standard options being agent, "
"admin,\n"
"  and customer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:451
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Webhooks\n"
": Webhooks are an easy way for systems to communicate with each\n"
"  other and allow you to send real-time data to any other application.\n"
"  We use them to allow our users to inform a third-party system about new\n"
"  information in Zammad.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:454
#, markdown-text
msgid "Zammad : Zammad is the greatest helpdesk in the world. Period."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:455
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "</VPGlossary>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: src/en/reference/index.md:1
#, no-wrap
msgid "Reference"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:1 src/en/reference/privacy.md:6
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Data Retention and External Services"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:10
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"How long does Zammad store user data? How can I manage its user data "
"retention behavior? To which services connects Zammad?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:11
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Data Retention"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:14
#, markdown-text
msgid "The following data is stored locally on the production system:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:15
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Tickets and Users"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:18
#, markdown-text
msgid "By default, Zammad never automatically deletes tickets or users."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:22
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To enable **automatic** deletion of tickets after a given interval,\n"
"use Zammad's scheduler. You can configure it in Zammad's admin interface "
"under\n"
"_Manage > Scheduler_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:27
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To **manually** delete users and all their associated tickets (e.g. in\n"
"compliance with a \"Right to Forget\" request under the GDPR), you can use\n"
"the data privacy functions in the admin panel under _System > Data "
"Privacy_\n"
"or [use the console](/en/reference/console#deleting-records).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:28
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Chat sessions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:32
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Once a chat session has been marked **closed**, it is scheduled for "
"automatic deletion 12 months later."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:36
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"IP address logs for chat sessions can be manually deleted by following the "
"steps in the [chat section in the "
"console](/en/reference/console#remove-ip-address-logs)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:37
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "CTI caller log"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:41
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"The caller log shows only the 60 most recent entries. Each entry in the "
"caller log is automatically deleted after 12 months."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:42
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Log files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:45
#, markdown-text
msgid "Zammad writes log files to disk (typically under `/opt/zammad/log/`)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:49
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Package installations will set up a separate system utility called "
"`logrotate` to rename and archive (or _rotate_) log files on a nightly basis "
"and remove old logs after 14 days."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:53
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"If installing from source, it is strongly recommended to configure "
"`logrotate` or a similar log management utility; Zammad will not purge old "
"logs on its own."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:54
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "User sessions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:58
#, markdown-text
msgid "Zammad maintains session information about every user currently logged in."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:63
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This information is automatically purged when a user logs out, and can\n"
"be viewed or manually deleted via the admin panel (under _System >\n"
"Sessions_). Users may also delete their own session information via the\n"
"user preferences menu, under _Devices_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:66
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Session information includes IP address (and possibly geographic location), "
"browser, time of original login, and time of last visit."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:67
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Data Privacy Tasks"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:71
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Each entry in the data privacy task list is automatically deleted after 12 "
"months."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:72
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "External Services"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:78
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Zammad utilizes third-party web services for certain functions, meaning\n"
"that user data may occasionally be sent or exposed to third parties.\n"
"These functions can be individually disabled in the admin panel under\n"
"_Settings > System > Services_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced div block (info)
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:79
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"By default, the third-party services that Zammad relies on are mostly\n"
"ones hosted and managed by the Zammad Foundation itself, but Zammad\n"
"can be extended to interface with other services instead.\n"
"\n"
"The source code for these third-party service integrations can be\n"
"found\n"
"[here](https://github.com/zammad/zammad/tree/develop/lib/service){target=_blank}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:89
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Images"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:93
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"No private images or personally-identifying information are stored on "
"images.zammad.com."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:99
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"The Images service caches publicly-available images from sources like "
"Gravatar and serves them to the Zammad application as user avatars and "
"organization logos. These images are discovered using MD5 digests of user "
"email addresses and organization domain names. User avatars are cached for 7 "
"days; organization logos are cached for 30 days."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:100
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "GeoCalendar"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:103 src/en/reference/privacy.md:112
#, markdown-text
msgid "No user information is stored or cached on geo.zammad.com."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:108
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"As part of its service-level agreement (SLA) functionality, Zammad requires "
"detailed, localized calendar information (_e.g.,_ to set the time zone and "
"accommodate national holidays and daylight savings time).  The GeoCalendar "
"service is used to retrieve this information."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:109
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "GeoIP"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:118
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"One of Zammad's security features is to track user sessions based on the "
"user's browser and country of origin. Suspicious login activity from a "
"different browser or country may trigger Zammad to dispatch an alert email "
"to the affected user. The GeoIP service is used to associate IP addresses to "
"a geographic origin."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:119
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Geolocation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:126
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Zammad's geolocation service relies on OpenStreetMap (OSM) unless you turned "
"it off. If you provide an address (or parts of an address) in a user object, "
"there is a lookup of coordinates from OSM which are stored in Zammad's "
"database. Have a look at their [privacy "
"policy](https://osmfoundation.org/wiki/Privacy_Policy){target=_blank} for "
"more information."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:12
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Some attributes of articles might not be straight forward or come with "
"fairly many options - below list hopefully helps you on this journey."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:18
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Zammad supports `text/html` for HTML formatted text or `text/plain` for "
"plain text. This allows you to have better formatting options if you need "
"them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:20
#, markdown-text
msgid "Zammad web UI usually uses `text/html`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:21
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`type`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:26
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Zammad supports a huge number of article types. Below list may be incomplete "
"depending on your instance and possibly installed add-ons / custom changes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:29
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"If not stated otherwise, all article types below are **communication "
"articles** and thus affecting SLA calculation in Zammad defaults."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:33
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"The difference is that communication articles provide the option to reply "
"automatically. Which actions exactly are available depends on the article "
"type and e.g. recipient lists."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:37
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`email`\n"
": This allows you to create incoming or outgoing email articles.\n"
"  However, this highly depends on the chosen `sender`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:40
#, markdown-text
msgid "`phone` : Indicates phone notes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:44
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`web`\n"
": Usually used by customers only. This type is being used when ever your\n"
"  customer uses the web UI to create articles.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:48
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`note`\n"
": When ever a communication does not fit (e.g.: internal notes) choose\n"
"  note. Zammad also uses this article type as default fall back.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:50
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "  This is **not a communication article**.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:53
#, markdown-text
msgid "`sms` : This type is being used for Zammad's SMS integration."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:57
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`chat`\n"
": This article type is technically a place holder and is only available\n"
"  via API.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:61
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`fax`\n"
": This article type is technically a place holder and is only available\n"
"  via API.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:66
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`twitter status` & `twitter direct-message`\n"
": These articles types are used by Zammad's twitter channel. Technically\n"
"  you can use these to automatically respond to existing requests via\n"
"  twitter.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:71
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`facebook feed post` & `facebook feed comment`\n"
": These articles types are used by Zammad's facebook channel. Technically\n"
"  you can use these to automatically respond to existing requests via\n"
"  facebook.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:75
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`telegram personal-message`\n"
": Used by Zammad's Telegram channel. Technically you can use these to\n"
"  automatically respond to existing requests via Telegram.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:81
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"This attribute allows you to set the visibility of your articles. For "
"internal visible only use `true`, for visibly for your customers as well use "
"`false`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced div block (warning)
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:82
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Visibility: internal doesn't mean it's silent**\n"
"\n"
"If you set an article to `internal: true` but choose to send an email,\n"
"please be aware that said Email is still being sent out!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:89
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`sender`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:92
#, markdown-text
msgid "Indicates which use did create the article. You can choose from:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
